The problem asks for the minimum number of operations to make a string of 'B's (black) and 'W's (white) entirely 'W's. An operation consists of choosing a substring of length $k$ and turning all characters in it to 'W'.

Let's analyze the operation. We want to cover all 'B's with intervals of length $k$. Since the operation turns cells to 'W', the order doesn't strictly matter for the final state, but we want to minimize the number of intervals. This is equivalent to covering all indices $i$ where $s[i] == 'B'$ with the minimum number of intervals of length $k$.

Let the indices of the black cells be $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_m$ in increasing order. We need to select a set of intervals $[l_j, r_j]$ such that $r_j - l_j + 1 = k$ and for every $i$, there exists some $j$ such that $p_i \in [l_j, r_j]$. We want to minimize the number of intervals.

This looks like a greedy problem. We have a set of points (black cells) on a line. We want to cover them with segments of fixed length $k$.
Consider the leftmost black cell at index $p_1$. To cover this cell, we must choose an interval that includes $p_1$. To maximize the utility of this interval (i.e., to cover as many subsequent black cells as possible), we should extend the interval as far to the right as possible.
So, the optimal interval covering $p_1$ should start at some index $l$ such that $l \le p_1 \le l+k-1$. To cover the most to the right, we want $l+k-1$ to be as large as possible. The constraint is that the interval must cover $p_1$, so $l \le p_1$. The maximum right endpoint is $p_1 + k - 1$. Thus, we should pick the interval $[p_1 - (k-1), p_1]$? No, wait.
The interval is defined by its start position or end position. Let's say we pick an interval $[i, i+k-1]$. If we want to cover $p_1$, we need $i \le p_1 \le i+k-1$. This implies $p_1 - k + 1 \le i \le p_1$.
To cover as many subsequent black cells as possible, we want the interval to end as far right as possible. The rightmost end is $p_1 + k - 1$. This corresponds to choosing the interval starting at $p_1$.
Wait, is it always optimal to start the interval at the position of the first uncovered black cell?
Let's check. Suppose the first uncovered black cell is at index $i$. We need to cover it. Any interval covering $i$ must be of the form $[j, j+k-1]$ where $j \le i \le j+k-1$. The rightmost point covered by such an interval is $j+k-1$. Since $j \le i$, the maximum rightmost point is when $j=i$, giving $i+k-1$.
So, the greedy strategy is: find the first black cell at index $i$. Place an interval of length $k$ starting at $i$ (covering $[i, i+k-1]$). This covers all black cells in this range. Then find the next uncovered black cell and repeat.

Let's trace this with an example.
